        <p>STOP 3 / 11 - THE FLOW IS DECLARED</p>
      </content>
    </shape>
    <shape type="text" topLeftX="56" topLeftY="30" width="848" height="34">
      <content textType="title" fontSize="24" fontFamily="Inter" bold="true" color="rgba(15,36,64,1)" wrap="false" textAlign="left">
        <p>Flow declared: order is configuration, not code</p>
      </content>
    </shape>
    <line startX="56" startY="64" endX="904" endY="64">
      <border color="rgb(39, 121, 167)" width="2"/>
    </line>
    <shape type="rect" topLeftX="56" topLeftY="84" width="532" height="384">
      <fill>
        <fillColor color="rgba(247,249,251,1)"/>
      </fill>
      <border color="rgb(214, 222, 232)" width="1"/>
    </shape>
    <shape type="text" topLeftX="72" topLeftY="100" width="504" height="352">
      <content textType="body" fontSize="11" fontFamily="Roboto Mono" color="rgba(61,74,92,1)" lineSpacing="multiple:1.32" textAlign="left">
        <p>&lt;chain name="payment_process"&gt;</p>
        <p>  &lt;node id="validate"    class="PaymentValidateNode"/&gt;</p>
        <p>  &lt;node id="idempotency" class="IdempotencyCheckNode"/&gt;</p>
        <p>  &lt;node id="assemble"    class="BankRequestAssembleNode"/&gt;</p>
        <p>  &lt;node id="initiate"    class="PaymentInitiateNode"/&gt;</p>
        <p>  &lt;node id="bank-invoke" class="BankInvokeNode"/&gt;</p>
        <p>  &lt;node id="parse"       class="BankResponseParseNode"/&gt;</p>
        <p>  &lt;node id="finalize"    class="PaymentFinalizeNode"/&gt;</p>
        <p>  &lt;node id="audit"       class="PaymentAuditNode"/&gt;</p>
        <p>&lt;/chain&gt;</p>
        <p>// payment-flow.xml - loaded by LiteFlow</p>
      </content>
    </shape>
    <shape type="text" topLeftX="604" topLeftY="92" width="300" height="250">
      <content textType="body" fontSize="13.5" fontFamily="Inter" color="rgba(61,74,92,1)" lineSpacing="multiple:1.4" textAlign="left">
        <ul>
          <li><p>The order of operations is data, not code - reorder without recompiling.</p></li>
          <li><p>Open / Closed: a new concern is a new node joined to the chain; existing nodes stay untouched.</p></li>
          <li><p>Honest trade-off: an external DSL is invisible to static analysis - we compensate with one class per node and tests.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </content>
    </shape>
    <shape type="rect" topLeftX="604" topLeftY="360" width="300" height="108">
      <fill>
        <fillColor color="rgba(232,241,247,1)"/>
      </fill>
    </shape>
    <shape type="text" topLeftX="616" topLeftY="374" width="276" height="84">
      <content textType="body" fontSize="13" fontFamily="Inter" color="rgba(15,36,64,1)" lineSpacing="multiple:1.35" textAlign="left">
        <p>Why it matters: the sequence of a payment is a product decision - keeping it visible keeps it reviewable.</p>
      </content>
    </shape>
    <shape type="text" topLeftX="856" topLeftY="510" width="48" height="18">
      <content textType="caption" fontSize="12" fontFamily="Inter" color="rgba(138,148,166,1)" textAlign="right" wrap="false">
        <p>09 / 24</p>
      </content>
    </shape>
  </data>
  <note>
    <content textType="body">
      <p>The order of the eight steps is not hard-coded anywhere - it lives in one XML file that LiteFlow loads at startup. Why do it this way? Because the sequence of a payment is a product decision: where validation happens, where idempotency is checked, when we write the record. Declaring it as configuration makes it visible and reviewable, and adding a new step means adding a node, not editing the old ones. The trade-off - an external DSL the IDE cannot trace - is paid back by one class per node and tests that pin the behavior.</p>
    </content>
  </note>
